# Chapter 6, part 6: the errors of a document that Print and Preview find.
# It holds the section "Print errors" [print-errors]: the Error Log in the
# pages, the Print Error markers in the text, the messages, what a failed
# print does (the banner, the marks, the Viewer's red status) and what a print
# that makes no file does.  The Print Error marker has its own entry in
# chapter 5 (markers-errors) and the search method Find Next Error is in
# chapter 3 (edit-c-find-error).

.h2 Print errors [print-errors]
.ix Print errors
.ix Error Log
.ix Error Log?
.ix Print Error markers
.ix Find Next Error
.ix Errors of a document
.ix Diagnostics
.ix Warnings of a print
.ix PRINT FAILED
.ix Banner
A document that cannot be printed as it is, is wrong; the program is not.  An
equation that is not TeX math, a picture whose file is not there, a character
that no font has: Print and Preview still make their pages (the piece that
cannot be set is printed as text, so that the PDF is always made), and they
report what is wrong and where.  A print that has errors is a {b:failed print}: its
pages are made, but they carry a large banner that says so ({n:print-failed}).
What is wrong is shown in several places at once, so that nothing has to
be written down:
.table 17 43
Where | What it holds
The banner | A line of large bold red capitals on every page of a failed
 print: "PRINT FAILED - 3 ERRORS - THIS PRINT IS INACCURATE" (
 {n:print-failed}).
The marks | A small red or orange label with the number of the entry of the
 Error Log, on the pages, at the place of each fault ({n:print-failed}).
The Error Log | A page group at the end of the PDF or of the Preview: a
 heading, the count, and one numbered entry for each error or warning, with
 the place in words ({n:print-errlog}).
Print Error markers | Markers in the text itself, one in front of each thing
 that is wrong, made by the Editor after a Print or a Preview (
 {n:print-errmarks}, and {n:markers-errors}).
The message line | How many were found, and the keys that go to them (
 {n:print-errline}).
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the same list goes to the standard error of the Printer and of
the Editor's print and preview modes, one line for each.
.endif
A clean document has none of
this: no banner, no mark, no Error Log, no empty page, no marker, and the
status of every program
stays what it was.

.h3 What is an error and what is a warning [print-errkinds]
.ix Error codes
.ix Print error codes
.ix equation-invalid
.ix picture-missing
.ix char-missing
An {b:error} is a piece of the document that the page could not take as it
was written: the page shows it in another form, or leaves it out.  A
{b:warning} is a piece that is printed, but not as the author may have
wished.  Each has a short code, which is written in the Error Log and in the
marker.  The messages are in plain English.
In the messages of the Include codes the file name is in quotation marks.
.table 16 6 28
Code | Kind | Message and what to do
equation-invalid | Error | "The equation is not valid TeX math, so it is
 printed as text."  The text of the Equation marker is not TeX.  Rewrite it
 in TeX ({n:equations}).
equation-refused | Error | "TeX rejects the equation (REASON), so it is printed
 as text."  The text reads as TeX math but LaTeX stopped at it, for example
 "Undefined control sequence.".  Correct the formula.
equation-empty | Warning | "The equation is empty."  Fill it in or delete the
 marker.
equation-unreadable | Error | "The equation cannot be read: it is too long or
 damaged, so it is printed as text."  Shorten the text of the equation.
char-missing | Warning | "No font has the character U+XXXX; it is printed as
 [U+XXXX]."  Use a character that the font has.  Told once for each such
 character in a line of text.
picture-noname | Error | "The picture marker has no file name."  Give the
 marker a file name.
picture-missing | Error | "Picture file not found: NAME (looked in PLACES)."
 Put the file where the document is, or correct the name.  The page has a red
 box with the words in the place of the picture.
picture-unreadable | Error | "The picture file NAME cannot be read: REASON."
 Correct the permissions of the file.
picture-type | Error | "The picture file NAME is of a type that cannot be
 printed (only PNG, JPEG, PDF and PostScript can)."  Convert the file.
picture-damaged | Error | "The picture file NAME is incomplete or damaged."
 Make the file again.
picture-refused | Error | "The picture cannot be used (REASON), so it is printed
 as text."  TeX refused the file.  Or, with no reason, "TeX stopped without
 saying why, so no picture is printed."
picture-link | Error | "The picture file cannot be given to TeX here."  The
 picture is printed as text.
marker-unsupported | Warning | "NAME markers are not typeset by Strata; the
 marker is printed as text."  The marker is printed in its long form.  See
 {x:markers-printed}.
latex-marker-off | Warning | "LaTeX markers are not allowed in this print: the
 text of this one is not written and the marker is printed as text."  The
 setting {c:latex_markers} is off ({n:appcfg-key-latex-markers}).  Turn it on to
 print the instructions.
latex-marker-unreadable | Error | "The text of this LaTeX marker is too long or
 damaged, so it is not written and the marker is printed as text."  Shorten the
 text to under 65000 characters ({n:marker-latex}).
marker-incell | Warning | "A page break or column break inside a table or a
 line with tabs cannot be honored; it is printed as text."  Move the marker.
symbol-unknown | Warning | "The special symbol is not known to Strata; it is
 printed as text."
xref-unresolved | Warning | "The cross-reference points at a section that is
 not in the document (or not in this part of it)."  A Preview of a part
 may say this about a section that the part does not hold.
counter-unresolved | Warning | "The value of the counter cannot be worked out;
 it is printed as text."
counter-limit | Warning | "A counter has reached 4294967295 and stays there;
 its later uses show the same number."  Said once for a document.
keep-unmatched | Error | "This End Keep has no Start Keep before it."
keep-doubled | Error | "A Keep was started while another is still open; the
 first has no End Keep."
keep-unclosed | Error | "This Start Keep has no End Keep after it."
if-unclosed | Error | "This If has no Endif after it."
else-unmatched | Error | "This Else has no If before it."
else-doubled | Error | "This If already has an Else."
endif-unmatched | Error | "This Endif has no If before it."
if-empty | Error | "This If has no expression: it needs a variable, an
 operator and a value, such as COLOR = red."  The branch is left out.
if-no-variable | Error | "This If does not begin with the name of a variable:
 a name starts with a letter."
if-bad-operator | Error | "The operator of this If is missing or not known:
 the operators are = # < > <= >=, and # means not equal."  (Another message
 shows that <> and == are not operators.)
if-no-literal | Error | "This If has nothing after the operator to compare the
 variable with."
if-bad-literal | Error | "The value of this If is not allowed", with the
 reason: a quotation mark or a character that is not printable ASCII.
if-too-long | Error | "The variable name of this If is longer than 255
 characters", or the value.
if-too-deep | Error | "These Ifs are nested too deep: the limit is ten."
if-structure | Warning | "Text that depends on an If cuts across the
 sections: a section whose parent is left out moves up a level."
include-noname | Error | "Invalid Include request: The Include marker has no
 file name."  A notice is printed in the place of the marker
 ({n:marker-include-errors}).
include-missing | Error | "Invalid Include request: The file NAME cannot be
 found."  The name is looked for beside the document and in the working
 directory ({n:marker-include-print}).
include-unreadable | Error | "Invalid Include request: The file NAME cannot be
 read."
include-damaged | Error | "Invalid Include request: The file NAME is not a
 Strata document or is damaged."
include-loop | Error | "Invalid Include request: The file NAME includes
 itself, directly or through other parts."  The second include is not made.
include-deep | Error | "Invalid Include request: The parts are nested more
 than 8 levels deep."
include-place | Error | "Invalid Include request" with one of three reasons:
 the marker is not alone in its block, it is in a headline, or a part with
 sections is at a marker that is not in the last block of its section.
tex-error | Error | "LaTeX reports: MESSAGE."  An error that LaTeX itself
 found and that none of the checks above explains.  One for each "!" line of
 the log, up to 100.  The place is the block that the line of the TeX file
 came from, or "line N of the file that TeX read" when that is not known.
.end
At most 200 are kept for one run.  When there are more, the count reports this
("more were found than are listed") and the Error Log ends with "N more
diagnostics were found and not listed."

.h3 The place [print-errplace]
.ix Position of an error
.ix Place of an error
Every entry names the place in the document in words.  The form is
.code
Section 3.1.1 Definition, block 2, line 4, Equation marker
.end
that is: the section number and headline, the block counted within that
section from 1 (the headline is block 1), the column (only for a block of
columns: "column 2"), the
line of the block, and, when the fault is in a marker, its name and the word
marker.  Text before the first section is "Title page, block N"; a block
outside any section is "Block N".  The line is the line of the block as it
was typed: the lines that are made when the text is shown are not counted.  A
fault that has no place at all is told as "no position".

.h3 The Error Log [print-errlog]
.ix Error Log, page
.ix Error Log?
.ix Error Log, Preview
When a run has errors or warnings, the PDF (or the pages of a Preview)
end with a new page that has the heading {b:Error Log} (with the banner of
{n:print-failed} on it, when the print failed), a line such as "3
errors and 1 warning found in this document.  Each entry gives the place in
the document, so that it can be fixed in the Editor.", and then one entry
for each fault, in the order of the document:
.table 12 48
Part of the entry | What it shows
Number and kind | "1. Error." or "3. Warning." in bold.
The message | The text of the table of {x:print-errkinds}.
Position | "Position:" and the place of {x:print-errplace}.
Text | "Text:" and the text that is wrong, in a typewriter font, cut to 72
 characters: the words of the equation, the file name of the picture, the
 character as U+XXXX.  Left out when there is none.
.end
For the example document of the tests, the last page of the PDF reads
.code
Error Log
3 errors and 1 warning found in this document.
1. Error. The equation is not valid TeX math, so it is printed as text.
   Position: Section 1 Purpose, block 3, line 2, Equation marker
   Text: the sum of a and b
2. Error. Picture file not found: pics/missing.pcx (looked in /work).
   Position: Section 2 Definition, block 3, line 1, Picture marker
   Text: pics/missing.pcx
.end
.pic pictures/print-errlog.png 5 The Error Log, the last page of the PDF of the test document: the banner above it, then three errors and one warning, each with its place.
The page carries the page number that the document's own numbering gives it
(the title page has none).  The log is the last page
of the part that was printed: the pages of the document before it are the
same, with or without it, and a Preview of a block or of a section has a log
for that part only.
.table 18 12 20
Field | Values | Meaning
Error Log? | Yes (default), No | In the Draft, Final and Preview Document
 panels.  Yes ends the PDF or the pages with the Error Log when there is
 something to say.  No leaves the page out.
.end
With No the faults are still marked in the document and counted on the message
line, and the banner and the marks of {x:print-failed} stay, with the numbers
that the entries would have had.  Preview Block and Preview Section have no
panel and always have the log.
The markers in the text are described in {x:print-errmarks}.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the option {c:--no-error-log} of the Printer and of the Editor's
print and preview modes does the same ({n:print-cmdline}).
.endif

.h3 The Print Error markers [print-errmarks]
.ix Print Error marker, made by Print
.ix Markers, Print Error
After a Print (Draft or Final) or a Preview of the Editor has typeset the
document, the Editor reads what the Printer found and puts a Print Error
marker into the text in front of each fault.  Only the Editor makes them: no
key, menu or command lets the user make one, and a document keeps them until
they are fixed and removed.  The marker has its own entry in {n:markers-errors}:
its fields, how it looks, that it is not printed, and how it is saved.
How to go from one to the next is in {x:edit-c-find-error}.
.table 19 41
Rule | What happens
Where the marker goes | In front of the character for a character that no
 font has; in front of the marker (equation, picture, keycap, Keep ...) that
 is at fault, found by its kind and its text; else at the start of the line
 of the block.  A place that cannot be found (the document was changed after
 the run) goes to the nearest place that can: the block, else the start of
 the section, else the start of the part that was printed.  The message then
 ends with "(The exact place was not found; this is the nearest.)".
A new run | First takes out the old markers of the same kind of run (Print or
 Preview) in the blocks that were printed, then puts the new ones in, so
 they do not pile up.  A run that finds nothing takes them out, and shows "no
 print errors".  The markers of the other kind of run, and those outside the
 part that was printed, stay.
A run that fails | Changes no marker ({n:print-failed}).
A Preview of a block or of a section | Marks and replaces only the blocks of
 that part.  What a Print of the whole document found elsewhere stays.
Cancelled | A run that was stopped with {k:Esc} changes no marker.
The file of the document | Not touched by a print; the document in the Editor
 is marked as changed (the star in the status line), because markers were
 put in.  {k:Ctrl-S} writes the markers with the text and they load again;
 Remove All of the Print menu takes them out first if they are not wanted.
.end
A marker that has been put in is taken out with
{k:Del}, with Remove or with Remove All of Print, Errors
({n:edit-c-find-error}).

.h3 The message line after a print [print-errline]
.ix Message line, print errors
.ix Print error, count
.ix No print errors
The text of the message line depends on how the run ended.
.table 33 27
Message | When
PRINT FAILED: Wrote /work/plan.pdf (3 pages).  3 errors and 1 warning. |
 A Print with no queue named, with errors.  The pages have the banner and
 the marks, and the markers are in the text.  With warnings only the text is
 "Wrote /work/plan.pdf (3 pages).  The document has 0 errors and 1
 warning." and nothing is marked.
Wrote /work/plan.pdf (12 pages)  No print errors. | A Print with no queue
 named, and none found.
Print: PRINT FAILED, 4 print errors (Ctrl-F8 finds the next) | After the box
 of a Print that used {c:lp}, with errors.  The box holds the text of the first
 row of this table with the answer of {c:lp}, or the message that the PDF was not
 sent.  "1 print error" in the singular.  With warnings only, the words
 "PRINT FAILED, " are not there.
Print: no print errors | The same, with none.
Preview done: PRINT FAILED, 4 print errors (Ctrl-F8 finds the next) | A Preview
 that was shown, with errors.  With warnings only, the words "PRINT FAILED, "
 are not there.
Preview done: no print errors | A Preview that was shown, with none.
Print error 3 of 4: MESSAGE [code] | After {k:Ctrl-F8}, {k:Ctrl-F7} or
 {k:Ctrl-F9} landed on a marker.  {x:edit-c-find-error}.
Wrapped to the start.  Print error 1 of 4: ... | The same, after the search
 went round the end of the document.
Wrapped to the end.  Print error 4 of 4: ... | The same, backward.
No print errors | A search for the next error, or Remove All, when there is
 none.
Print Error removed | Remove took out the marker under the cursor.
The cursor is not on a Print Error | Remove with the cursor elsewhere.
4 print errors removed | Remove All took them out ("1 print error
 removed" in the singular).
PRINT FAILED: Preview shown in spite of errors of LaTeX: MESSAGE | The Viewer
 ended after a preview whose pages LaTeX made in spite of an error of its own.
.end
.pic pictures/print-failed-message.png 5 The message line after a Print with errors, at the foot of the Editor's window (it is cut at the width of the window; the markers are in the text).
Print and Preview count the number that the Printer told, not the number of
markers: the two are equal unless the document has more than 200
({n:print-errkinds}).

.h3 A failed print: the banner, the marks and the Viewer [print-failed]
.ix Failed print
.ix Print failed
.ix Banner, PRINT FAILED
.ix Failed Preview
.ix Marks of a failed print
.ix Inaccurate pages
A print that has {b:errors} is a {b:failed print}.  A print that has only
warnings is not: its pages are as good as can be made, and the Error Log shows
what was not.  A failed print still makes its PDF or its Preview (the piece
that cannot be set is printed as text), but the pages are not what the document
says, and nobody must take them for good pages.  So the pages say so, and so
do the Editor and the Viewer:
.table 13 47
Part | What happens
The banner | On {b:every} page, the page of the Error Log too, a line of large
 bold red capitals: "PRINT FAILED - 3 ERRORS - THIS PRINT IS INACCURATE" in a
 PDF, "PRINT FAILED - 3 ERRORS - THIS PREVIEW IS INACCURATE" in a Preview ("1
 ERROR" in the singular).  It stands in the top margin, three quarters of the
 way down it (not lower than 0.45 inch and not higher than 0.2 inch from the
 edge), so that it does not move or hide a line of the text: the pages are
 the same in number and in layout as without it.  Shrunk to fit if the page
 is narrow.  On a page that is turned (an orientation marker) it stands along
 the edge that is the top of the page as it is read.
The marks | A small label at each place that has a fault: white letters on red,
 {b:E3}, for an error, black letters on orange, {b:W3}, for a warning.  The
 number is that of the entry in the Error Log, which gives the fault (the
 label has the number also when the Error Log is left out).  It stands at the
 place that the Printer gave: in front of the character that no font has, in
 front of the equation, picture or other marker that is at fault, else at the
 start of the line or of the block.  A fault that has no place on a page (for
 example an error of LaTeX that cannot be traced to a block) has no label: the
 banner and the log show it.  The labels are in the pages only, never in the
 document.  A label is set in the line and takes a little room, so a line that
 holds one may break at another word than it would; the page is inaccurate
 anyway, and the label is the sign of it.
The Viewer | When the print failed, the Viewer shows a
 {b:red area} of its own above its status bar, with white bold letters: "PRINT
 FAILED - 3 errors - THIS PREVIEW IS INACCURATE" ({n:print-viewer-status}).
 The Viewer only shows it and makes no mark.
The messages | The messages of the Printer and of the Editor begin or contain
 "PRINT FAILED" ({n:print-errline} and {n:print-strprint}).
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the status of a failed print is unchanged, 4 for the Printer and
5 for the print mode of the Editor.  The word is added to the text, not to the
numbers.
.endif
.pic pictures/print-failed-page.png 5 A page of a failed print (the test document): the banner in the top margin, and the marks E1, E2, W3 and E4 in front of the equation, the picture, the character and the second equation.
A clean print has none of this: no banner, no label and no red area.  The
Error Log does not matter: the
option that turns it off ({n:print-errlog}) leaves the banner and the marks
as they are.
.table 10 8 32
Option | Default | Meaning
Banner | On | The banner on every page of a failed print.  The Print and
 Preview panels have no field for it: they always make it.  The marks stay
 when the banner is left out.
Error Log? | Yes | Does not change the banner or the marks.
.end
In a Preview with errors the same happens in the pages, and the Editor's
message line reports this ({n:print-errline}).  A Preview of a clean document, or
one made without the banner, has no red area.

.h3 A print that makes no file [print-nofile]
.ix Print that made nothing
.ix Print, no file made
A print can also make {b:no file at all}: LaTeX stopped and left none, the
Printer or {c:pdflatex} is missing, the document could not be read, the
directory could not be written.  That is not a failed print in the sense of
{x:print-failed}, because there are no pages to carry a banner; it is a
failure of the program.  Strata then does exactly this, and nothing more:
.table 20 40
Part | What happens
The screen | A box titled Print, with the message of the failure: "LaTeX
 failed: ! TEXT l.12 THE LINE" (the first error line of the TeX log and the
 line after it) or "Print failed: ..." or one of the other messages of
 {x:print-messages}.  A Preview has no box: the message line shows "LaTeX
 failed: ..." or "Preview failed: ...".
The PDF | None is written; one that was there before stays as it was.
The Error Log | None.  There is no PDF to hold it.
The banner and the marks | None: there are no pages.
The markers | No marker is put in, taken out or changed: a run that did not
 get as far as the document tells nothing about it.  The count line of
 {x:print-errline} is not shown.
.end
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: with no pages the exit status is 1 for the Printer and for the
print mode of the Editor.
.endif
A document whose pages were made with errors is the other case, and its
messages are those of {x:print-errline}.

.h3 Export a LaTeX File and errors [print-errexport]
.ix Export a LaTeX File, errors
The Error Log, the banner and the marks belong to the pages of a print.  Export
a LaTeX File ({n:file-export-latex}) finds the same faults, but it writes the
file without them: the message line gives their number and a box lists the
first of them, and no Print Error marker is put in the document.

.h3 Print and Preview with no document to mark [print-errtext]
.ix Print Document, errors
.ix Preview Document, errors
.ix Exit status 5
The print and preview modes of the Editor have no open document and so no
marker.  They write the faults as text at the end of the
run, under the line "FILE: the document has errors or warnings:" (followed,
when there are errors, by the line "PRINT FAILED: the output was made, but it
is inaccurate."; a preview says "the preview is inaccurate"): one numbered
line for each (error or warning, the place, the message with its code in
square brackets, the text in quotation marks) and a last line "PRINT FAILED: 3
errors, 1 warning" (without the words "PRINT FAILED: " when there are warnings
only).
The PDF is made all the same.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: the exit status of the print and preview modes of the Editor is 5
when the document made its output and has faults ({n:print-cli}); a clean
document keeps status 0.
.endif

.note The Error Log of a Preview is the same page, with the word PREVIEW in the
  banner.
